Docker recap:
- Docker helps package app + deps into containers
- image = packaged environment
- container = running instance of image
- containers are lightweight because they use host OS kernel

Images vs Containers:
- image → read-only blueprint
- container → image + thin writable layer
- every container always comes from an image
- multiple containers can be created from same image

Dockerfile:
- plain text file with instructions to build an image
- Docker reads instructions top to bottom
- every instruction creates a new layer

FROM:
- defines base image
- mandatory in every Dockerfile
- base image provides OS + tools
- best practice → use minimal images (slim, alpine)
- avoid `latest`, pin versions

Building images:
- `docker build -t imagename .`
- `-t` → tag (image name)
- image names must be lowercase
- last `.` → build context
- images stored internally at `/var/lib/docker`

How Docker builds image:
- reads Dockerfile
- pulls base image (if not cached)
- executes instructions one by one
- creates layers for each instruction
- merges layers into final image

Build time vs Run time:
- build phase → FROM, RUN, COPY, ADD, ENV, WORKDIR
- run phase → CMD, ENTRYPOINT
- important to know what runs when

RUN:
- executes commands at build time
- used for installing packages, setup
- each RUN creates a new layer
- best practice → combine commands using `&&`
- clean cache to reduce image size

CMD:
- default command when container starts
- only last CMD is considered
- can be overridden at runtime
- example: `docker run image ls` overrides CMD

ENTRYPOINT:
- main executable of container
- not easily overridden
- container always runs ENTRYPOINT

ENTRYPOINT + CMD:
- ENTRYPOINT → command
- CMD → default arguments
- CMD can be overridden, ENTRYPOINT stays

COPY vs ADD:
- COPY → simple, predictable file copy
- ADD → extra features (URL download, tar extract)
- best practice → use COPY unless ADD is needed

Multi-stage builds:
- use multiple FROM instructions
- build app in one stage (e.g. JDK)
- run app in final stage (e.g. JRE)
- reduces image size significantly

Copy-on-write (CoW):
- image layers are read-only
- container writes go to writable layer
- improves performance and storage efficiency

Performance optimizations:
- reduce number of layers
- choose small base images
- use JRE instead of JDK in production
- install deps before copying full code for caching

Useful commands:
- `docker images`
- `docker history <image>`
- `docker inspect <image>`
- `docker scan <image>`
- `docker rmi <image-id>`

Example Dockerfile idea:
- lightweight base image
- install deps early
- ENTRYPOINT defines main command
- CMD provides default args

Key takeaway:
- Dockerfile defines how image is built
- docker build → creates image
- docker run → creates + runs container
- ENTRYPOINT vs CMD distinction is critical
